import{_ as t,o,c as a,a2 as i}from"./chunks/framework.Cdp8-jB6.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"ADR-089 — Super-circular loft/skip model for lifting atmospheric entry","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"adr/ADR-089.md","filePath":"adr/ADR-089.md"}'),r={name:"adr/ADR-089.md"};function s(n,e,l,c,h,d){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<h1 id="adr-089-—-super-circular-loft-skip-model-for-lifting-atmospheric-entry" tabindex="-1">ADR-089 — Super-circular loft/skip model for lifting atmospheric entry <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-089-—-super-circular-loft-skip-model-for-lifting-atmospheric-entry" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;ADR-089 — Super-circular loft/skip model for lifting atmospheric entry&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Status · Accepted · 2026-08-31 · Operator-directed (Physics Lab spaceflight program) · builds on ADR-088 (range-control entry) + RFC-036 (2-DOF descent) · unblocks Mars guided-lifting entries and the lunar-return 11 km/s skip (#29 Phases 2–3)</p></blockquote><p><strong>Gating sentence.</strong> The descent integrator (<code>descent-physics.ts</code>) gains a <strong>loft/skip capability</strong> for LIFTING super-circular entries: the 2-DOF γ integration is enabled when the vehicle has lift (not only for sub-circular entries), the γ-floor that forbade climbing is lifted for lifting bodies (so γ can go negative = loft), and an <strong>energy-conserving exo-atmospheric coast</strong> holds the specific orbital energy above the atmosphere so explicit Euler doesn&#39;t drift the skip arc. <code>skip_out</code> and <code>second_entry</code> beats mark the crossing — armed only after first atmosphere contact, so a capsule that merely ENTERS above the 105 km skip line (all of them do — interface ~122 km) does not fire a spurious beat. <strong>NON-LIFTING profiles are BYTE-IDENTICAL</strong> (no code path runs for <code>hasLift == false</code>). The lifting profiles that use this path: the 16 LEO Earth capsules (sub-circular — they shift a hair from the coast-hold drag between 122–105 km, e.g. apollo7 solved bank 0.604→0.577, but never loft: min γ ≈ 0.9°); the shipped <strong>apollo8</strong> lunar-return (10.8 km/s — genuinely lofts/skips); and the 3 <strong>Mars</strong> guided-lifting entries (super-circular, but steep enough at ~15° that they dig in, no skip). but they are not byte-identical.</p><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Context&quot;">​</a></h2><p>ADR-088 gave the entry integrator a downrange DOF + range-control bank, but capped γ at ~0.1° descending (never fly upward) and disabled the 2-DOF integration entirely for super-circular arrivals. That made two real cases unmodellable:</p><ul><li><strong>Lunar-return entry</strong> (Apollo CM at ~11 km/s): the corridor is a knife-edge and the vehicle lofts back above the atmosphere (the Apollo-4 double-g-pulse) before its final descent.</li><li><strong>Mars guided lifting entries</strong> (Curiosity/MSL, Perseverance, Tianwen-1): super-circular interplanetary arrivals that flew real bank-steered lifting entries — now (#29 Phase 2) modelled 2-DOF with lift at the real ~15° FPA + L/D 0.24 (MSL sourced), reading ~6.5–8.5 g (flown MSL ~12 g; the fixed-bank residual). Steep enough not to loft, so no skip — the skip path is exercised by the lunar-return case below.</li></ul><p>Measurement (ADR-088 Phase 1a) established that the γ-floor is irrelevant at LEO energy — Earth capsules don&#39;t loft there — so lifting it is safe for the LEO capsules and only activates the loft where the energy genuinely produces one (super-circular).</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Decision&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li><strong>Enable 2-DOF for lifting super-circular.</strong> <code>dynamicalGamma = (subCircular || hasLift) &amp;&amp; gammaIsDynamical(phase)</code>. Ballistic super-circular arrivals keep fixed-γ (unchanged).</li><li><strong>Lift the γ-floor for lifting bodies.</strong> <code>if (!hasLift &amp;&amp; γ &lt; 0.0017) γ = 0.0017</code>. A lifting entry may climb (γ&lt;0) — that IS the skip; a ballistic capsule still can&#39;t fly upward.</li><li><strong>Energy-conserving coast.</strong> Above <code>SKIP_ALT_M</code> (105 km) the air is negligible and explicit Euler drifts specific energy over a long arc. On entering the coast we record the specific orbital energy <code>E = v²/2 − μ/r</code> and each step correct <code>v = √(2·(E + μ/r))</code> — gravity is conservative, so the coast keeps E exactly, bounding the drift. Drag resumes on re-entry.</li><li><strong>Skip beats.</strong> <code>skip_out</code> when the capsule first crosses above <code>SKIP_ALT_M</code>; <code>second_entry</code> when it falls back below. (Fully hyperbolic exit — never returns — simply times out at <code>maxTS</code> as an honest mission failure; the too-shallow corridor edge.)</li></ol><p>Validated behaviour (Apollo CM aero, 11 km/s, full lift-up): 4° too-shallow → skips onto a huge bound ellipse (still climbing past 40,000 km at the <code>maxTS</code> cutoff; from the final specific energy its apogee is ~71,000 km — it WOULD return, hours later, but not before the sim window, so the run is an honest mission failure — NOT a hyperbolic escape); 5.5–6.5° corridor → the skip fires AFTER the first deceleration pulse (skip_out at ~176 s, first peak-g at ~68 s), lofts to ~160 km, and the coast holds specific energy to <strong>&lt; 1 %</strong> across the arc before the second entry lands it at <strong>4–8 g</strong> (real lunar return ~6.5–8 g); ≥7° or lift-down → digs in at 10–16 g. Tests in <code>descent-physics.test.ts</code> lock: the skip ORDER (skip_out after the first peak, second_entry after skip — non-vacuous), the energy-hold (&lt; 1 % drift), a LEO lifting reentry emitting NO skip beats (the entry-above-skip-line regression), the lift-up-vs-down g trade, and the too-shallow failure.</p><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Consequences&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Unblocks Phase 2 (Mars guided lifting) and Phase 3 (lunar-return + Apollo round-trip capstone)</strong> — the model can now fly the super-circular lifting entries those need.</li><li><strong>Non-lifting profiles (59 of the 75 shipped) are byte-identical</strong> — no code path runs for <code>hasLift == false</code>. The 16 lifting Earth capsules shift slightly (the coast-hold trims a little drag on the way in; apollo7 solved bank 0.604→0.577, well within test tolerance) but do not loft (they&#39;re sub-circular — γ never goes negative; min γ ≈ 0.9°). Descent + HUD suites green.</li><li><strong>The LEO lifting capsules&#39; γ-floor is genuinely inert</strong> — they never reach γ&lt;0 (measured), so lifting the floor changes nothing <em>dynamically</em> there; the small footprint shift above is the coast-hold, not lofting. Their ~1–1.5 g-above-flown residual (ADR-088 Phase 1a) is a separate fixed-bank/controller gap — and a measured negative result stands behind it: the naive decel-hold controller does NOT close it (it overshoots to ~6 g when targeting 3 g; a predictor-corrector would be needed). See ADR-088 Phase 1a note.</li></ul><h2 id="non-goals-honest-simplifications" tabindex="-1">Non-goals / honest simplifications <a class="header-anchor" href="#non-goals-honest-simplifications" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Non-goals / honest simplifications&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Crossrange / bank reversals</strong> — still out (planar model).</li><li><strong>The coast is Keplerian-energy-held, not a full two-body propagation</strong> — it conserves E but integrates the same planar EOM through the thin arc; fine for the teaching skip, not an ephemeris.</li><li><strong>A fully hyperbolic (escaping) entry</strong> is reported as a timeout failure, not a distinct &quot;escaped&quot; outcome — acceptable for the corridor lesson (too-shallow = lost).</li><li><strong>Not re-calibrating the LEO capsules</strong> — their peak-g residual is an active-g-limit-controller gap (ADR-088), not a loft gap.</li><li><strong>apollo8&#39;s modeled skip is FULLER than the flown one</strong> — our fixed-lift-up flies a genuine exo-atmospheric skip (apex ~136 km, ~357 s vacuum coast, ~26 min interface-to-splash), while the real Apollo 8 up-control modulated bank specifically to keep the loft INSIDE the atmosphere (apex ~60–65 km) and splashed down in roughly half the time. Same double-pulse <em>shape</em>, ~2× duration and a higher apex — the price of fixed lift-up vs real bank modulation. Peak-g 7.8 vs flown ~6.8. The Lab connection copy says &quot;note our model flies a fuller exo-atmospheric skip&quot;, not &quot;exactly this&quot;.</li><li><strong>Mars guided-lifting timeline runs long</strong> — Curiosity ~9.1 min entry-to-touchdown (chute at Mach ~2.0) vs the flown &quot;7 minutes of terror&quot;; same fixed-lift-up-vs-bank-modulation cause. Peak-g 6.7–8.5 g (flown MSL ~12 g). More physical than the old 1-DOF (which undershot the duration), but not a match — an honest teaching-grade lifting entry, not a validated MSL reconstruction.</li></ul>',14)])])}const g=t(r,[["render",s]]);export{p as __pageData,g as default};
